Consider a smooth function \(f : [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}\) given by
\[
f(x) = \cos{x} + \dfrac{1}{2} \cdot \dfrac{\sin{x}}{x^2}
\]
Find the value of \(r\) such that the following conditions hold:
\begin{align}
f(c) - f(0^+) &= 4.1r \\
f'(c) &= -0.1,
\end{align}
where \(c\) is a point in the interval \([a, b]\), and the convention is that \(0^+ \to 0\).

